Hello=20

I am a new to distance sampling and have a few questions regarding its us=
e even after having read Intro to Distance Sampling and the user manual a=
s well as browsing the forum. I have bird point transect data collected o=
ver 3 years with 2 to 3 visits each year for each of 67 points (although =
some points were not surveyed at all during the first year) stratified ov=
er 3 habitat types.  Observations were recorded as being in one of 4, 10 =
m wide distance intervals. My goal is to obtain point-level density estim=
ates for each of several bird species so that I can calculate density wit=
hin each habitat type.  I need point level density estimates as these dat=
a will be used in regression analyses.  To make things as simple as possi=
ble I have created text files for each species in each habitat type and w=
ill use year as a covariate. Below is an example subset for one species f=
or one habitat type.   The stratum layer is represtented by year, the sam=
ple layer contains point count labels and survey effort, and observation =
is the radial distance at the midpoint of the distance interval they were=
 recorded.  My questions are:1) Is this the correct way to set up the dat=
a?; 2) How do I account for variation in survey effort when building mode=
ls - do I obtain density estimates per point and then divide them myself =
by 2 or 3 or does distance do this for you?  I really appreciate anyone's=
 advice on this.=20=20=20=20

